  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating outcomes with probability > 1/n and then computing the average of those outcomes. In this case, under the strict interpretation, no outcome qualifies as probable, so the reason cannot be applied. Even under a relaxed interpretation (≥), both lotteries have an average of 500, so the reason would be indifferent. However, the individual clearly prefers Lottery B. Their justification is based on risk aversion and the psychological comfort of certainty, not on the average of probable outcomes. There is no mention of probabilities, outcome categorization, or averaging. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason to make the choice. The reasoning process described is fundamentally different—emotional and risk-based—rather than computational and outcome-based as required by the reason. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
